Subject: Remove The dtoa Exponent Cliff For Small Values

What this does
  js_dtoa's shortest-representation search hands an extreme negative decimal
  exponent to the platform conversion: it binary-searches precisions 1..17 and
  accepts a candidate only when it parses back to exactly this double, so the
  smallest accepted candidate is the shortest representation. The existing
  formatter is then fed the same (mant, P, E) the search would have produced.

Why we need it
  The search is multi-precision, and mul_pow builds radix^f by repeatedly
  multiplying or dividing the whole number, so its cost grows with the decimal
  exponent. Near the bottom of the double range that collapses: the smallest
  denormal cost about a hundred times an ordinary value.

What it adds
  The branch is taken only for JS_DTOA_FORMAT_FREE, radix 10 and E <= -100. No
  format, opcode or bytecode change. The positive side is left alone, where the
  platform round trip costs more than the engine's search when many digits are
  needed.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No

--- a/dtoa.c
+++ b/dtoa.c
@@ -1180,7 +1180,37 @@
        B^(P-1) <= F < 2.B^P. */
     E = 1 + mul_log2_radix(e - 1, radix);
     
